   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "For regression, `bag.oob_score_` is the OOB $R^2$ score. For classification, it is the OOB accuracy.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Random Forest Regressor\n",
    "\n",
    "A random forest regressor is similar, but the random feature selection is built in."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 3,
   "id": "5774dc3d",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestRegressor\n",
    "\n",
    "rf = RandomForestRegressor(\n",
    "    n_estimators=300,\n",
    "    max_features=1.0,\n",
    "    bootstrap=True,\n",
    "    oob_score=True,\n",
    "    random_state=0,\n",
    "    n_jobs=-1,\n",
    ")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "25f2492b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The most important additional argument is `max_features`.\n",
    "\n",
    "For example:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 4,
   "id": "b5c9df35",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "#| eval: false\n",
    "RandomForestRegressor(max_features=1.0)\n",
    "RandomForestRegressor(max_features=\"sqrt\")\n",
    "RandomForestRegressor(max_features=0.5)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "25597031",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "These mean:\n",
    "\n",
    "- `1.0`: consider all predictors at each split.\n",
    "- `\"sqrt\"`: consider about $\\sqrt p$ predictors at each split.\n",
    "- `0.5`: consider half of the predictors at each split.\n",
    "\n",
    "When `max_features=1.0`, a random forest regressor is very close to bagging with decision trees. When `max_features` is smaller, the trees become more decorrelated.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Useful Attributes\n",
    "\n",
    "After fitting a bagging or random forest model, several attributes are useful."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 5,
   "id": "a0c655d0",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "#| eval: false\n",
    "rf.oob_score_\n",
    "rf.feature_importances_\n",
    "rf.estimators_[0]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "6edafeff",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Here:\n",
    "\n",
    "- `.oob_score_` gives the OOB score if `oob_score=True`.\n",
    "- `.feature_importances_` gives impurity-based variable importance.\n",
    "- `.estimators_` stores the fitted trees.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Tuning Hyperparameters\n",
    "\n",
    "Bagging and random forests are usually easier to tune than boosting. Still, several parameters matter.\n",
    "\n",
    "| Parameter | Main effect |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| `n_estimators` | More trees reduce Monte Carlo variation, but increase computation. |\n",
    "| `max_features` | Controls tree correlation. Smaller values create more randomness. |\n",
    "| `max_samples` | Controls bootstrap sample size. Smaller values create more diversity. |\n",
    "| `min_samples_leaf` | Controls smoothness of each tree. Larger values reduce overfitting. |\n",
    "| `max_depth` | Limits tree depth. Often left as `None`, but can help noisy data. |\n",
    "\n",
    "A practical tuning strategy is:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Choose a reasonably large `n_estimators`.\n",
    "2. Tune `max_features` and `min_samples_leaf`.\n",
    "3. If the model still overfits, tune `max_samples` or `max_depth`.\n",
    "4. Use cross-validation or OOB error to compare choices.\n",
    "\n",
    "For random forests, `n_estimators` is usually not a regularization parameter in the usual sense. Increasing the number of trees usually stabilizes the model rather than causing overfitting. The main cost is computation.\n",
    "\n",
    "The following code uses cross-validation to tune a random forest regressor."
   ]
  },
